<h1>Alpha and Beta in Finance</h1>
<p class="lead">Why this matters: this repo's headline claim — the best model bundle made <strong>~27.19% annualized</strong> out-of-sample — is incomplete until you ask: <em>how much of that was just market exposure?</em> Alpha and beta are the decomposition that answers it, and they're literally the linear regression you derived in Lesson 0026, pointed at returns.</p>

<h2>1. The model: CAPM as a regression</h2>
<p>Regress your strategy's (excess) returns on the market's:</p>
<div class="callout"><p style="margin:0"><strong>r<sub>strategy,t</sub> = α + β · r<sub>market,t</sub> + ε<sub>t</sub></strong><sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-1">[1]</a></sup> &nbsp;— estimated by OLS, so &nbsp;<strong>β = cov(r_s, r_m) / var(r_m)</strong> &nbsp;and&nbsp; <strong>α = r̄_s − β·r̄_m</strong> — slope and intercept from Lesson 0026's one-feature formula, verbatim.</p></div>
<table>
<tr><th>Quantity</th><th>What it measures</th><th>Reading it</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>β (beta)</strong></td><td>Sensitivity to the market: on a day the market moves +1%, the strategy moves +β% on average</td><td>β=1: index-like · β&gt;1: amplified · 0&lt;β&lt;1: damped · β≈0: <strong>market-neutral</strong> · β&lt;0: hedge-like</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>α (alpha)</strong></td><td>The intercept — average return left over <em>after</em> the market's contribution is subtracted out (Jensen's alpha<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-2">[2]</a></sup>)</td><td>The part of performance you can't get by just buying the index with leverage. The thing quant funds are paid for.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>ε (residual)</strong></td><td>Day-to-day noise uncorrelated with the market</td><td>Its volatility is your <em>idiosyncratic risk</em>; diversification attacks this term</td></tr>
</table>
<p>(The textbook CAPM uses returns in excess of the risk-free rate, r − r_f. This repo's convention is r_f = 0 — same as its Sharpe in <code>analyze_equity.py</code> — so the simple form above is consistent.)</p>

<h3>Worked example</h3>
<p>Your backtest returns 20%/yr. SPY did 15%. Sounds like +5% of skill — but the regression says β = 1.3. Then:</p>
<ul>
<li>Market's contribution: β × r_m = 1.3 × 15% = <strong>19.5%</strong>.</li>
<li>α = 20% − 19.5% = <strong>+0.5%</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Almost the entire "outperformance" was just holding 1.3× of market risk — achievable with index futures for a few basis points. The strategy's actual skill content is half a percent, before asking whether even that is statistically distinguishable from zero (the α's t-stat, via the standard errors from Lesson 0026, answers that — and for most strategies on a few years of data, it isn't).</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Why anyone pays 2-and-20:</strong> leverage scales β for nearly free, so the market's return is a commodity. α is the scarce thing — uncorrelated return that improves a portfolio no matter what else is in it. "Hedge fund" originally meant exactly this: hedge out β, keep α.</div>

<h2>2. In this project</h2>
<h3><code>analyze_equity.py:compute_metrics</code> — what's missing and the 10-line fix</h3>
<p>The function reports CAGR, vol, Sharpe, MaxDD — all <em>absolute</em> metrics. Nothing compares the equity curve to a benchmark, so a bull-market backtest gets credit for the bull market. The addition would look like this <span class="illus">(illustrative — not yet in the repo)</span>:</p>
<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">alpha_beta</span>(strategy_ret: pd.Series, market_ret: pd.Series) -&gt; dict:
    <span class="com"># align the two return series on shared dates</span>
    df = pd.<span class="fn">concat</span>([strategy_ret, market_ret], axis=<span class="num">1</span>, keys=[<span class="str">"s"</span>, <span class="str">"m"</span>]).<span class="fn">dropna</span>()
    beta = df[<span class="str">"s"</span>].<span class="fn">cov</span>(df[<span class="str">"m"</span>]) / df[<span class="str">"m"</span>].<span class="fn">var</span>()      <span class="com"># Lesson 0026: slope = cov/var</span>
    alpha_daily = df[<span class="str">"s"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>() - beta * df[<span class="str">"m"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>()
    <span class="kw">return</span> {<span class="str">"alpha_annual"</span>: alpha_daily * <span class="num">252</span>, <span class="str">"beta"</span>: beta}
</pre>
<p>Feed it the walk-forward equity curve's <code>pct_change()</code> and SPY's daily returns over the same dates. Until that number exists, "27.19% annualized 2020–2025" is unresolved: 2020–2025 contained a historic bull run, and a long-only top-5 momentum-flavored portfolio plausibly carries β near 1. The honest brag is "X% alpha at β = Y", not total return.</p>

<h3>The exercise-03 connection: a hedge ratio <em>is</em> a beta</h3>
<p><code>hedge_ratio(y, x)</code> in <code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/pairs.py</code> computes cov/var of two <em>assets</em> — mathematically the same slope, asked a different question: "how much of x offsets one unit of y?" Pairs trading in Lesson 0018 is now expressible in one sentence of this lesson's vocabulary: <strong>short the pair-beta, harvest the spread's mean reversion as alpha.</strong> Likewise "market-neutral" from that lesson just means engineering portfolio β to ≈ 0 — e.g. holding the repo's top-5 longs against a short index position sized by the portfolio's measured β.</p>

<h3>Why a regulator-grade version uses the same machinery</h3>
<p>Everything here generalizes: replace the single market factor with several (size, value, momentum — Fama-French style<sup><a class="fn" href="#fn-3">[3]</a></sup>) and the multi-feature OLS of Lesson 0026 decomposes returns into many betas plus a residual alpha. Factor neutrality (Lesson 0018's glossary entry) = driving <em>those</em> betas to zero too. One regression, the whole risk-attribution industry.</p>

<h2>3. Often confused with</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h4>Alpha vs Total return</h4><p>Total return = α + β·(market) + luck. A 30% year with β = 2 in a 15% market is ZERO alpha. Conversely a +4% year with β = 0 in a −20% market is heroic. Never evaluate a strategy's return without asking what its β bought for free.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Alpha vs Sharpe</h4><p>Both are "skill" metrics with different denominators. <strong>Sharpe</strong> = return per unit of TOTAL volatility — it doesn't care whether the volatility is market or idiosyncratic. <strong>Alpha</strong> = return orthogonal to a BENCHMARK. A leveraged index fund has the same Sharpe as the index but zero alpha; a market-neutral book can have modest Sharpe and pure alpha. Allocators want both numbers.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Beta (CAPM) vs Beta (hedge ratio) vs β̂ (regression)</h4><p>All the same OLS slope wearing different hats. <strong>Regression β̂</strong>: generic coefficient. <strong>CAPM beta</strong>: that slope when x = market returns. <strong>Hedge ratio</strong>: that slope when x = the other leg of a pair, used to size the offset. When a quant says "beta" unqualified, context decides — usually CAPM.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h4>Market-neutral vs Hedged vs Flat</h4><p><strong>Flat</strong>: no positions. <strong>Hedged</strong>: some specific risk offset (maybe only partially). <strong>Market-neutral</strong>: measured portfolio β ≈ 0 — you can be massively invested long and short while having no net market exposure. Neutrality is a measured property, re-estimated continuously, not a label you set once.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>4. Practice</h2>
<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q1. Strategy returns 20%/yr with β = 1.3; the market returned 15%. (r_f = 0.) The alpha is…</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="+5% is the naive excess over the market — it ignores that β=1.3 means the strategy carried MORE than one market's worth of exposure.">+5% — it beat the market by five points.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Expected from market exposure alone: 1.3 × 15% = 19.5%. α = 20% − 19.5% = +0.5%. Nearly all the outperformance was leverage-equivalent market risk, which costs basis points to replicate with futures. Skill content: half a percent — probably not statistically distinguishable from zero on a few years of data.">+0.5% — the market explains 1.3 × 15% = 19.5% of the 20%.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="−6.5% would be 20% − 1.3×15% − 5% — mixing up the naive excess with the CAPM-adjusted one. Just subtract β×r_m once.">−6.5%.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Alpha is computable from exactly these inputs: α = r_s − β·r_m under the r_f=0 convention.">Can't be computed without the Sharpe ratio.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q2. β = cov(r_s, r_m)/var(r_m). Where have you literally seen this formula before in this repo's code?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. hedge_ratio() regresses one asset on another and returns the OLS slope — cov/var. CAPM beta is the same slope with the market as the regressor. One formula: generic regression slope (Lesson 0026), pair hedge ratio (exercise 03), CAPM beta (this lesson). Recognizing the same math across contexts is most of the trick.">In <code>solutions/03_stat_arb_risk/pairs.py:hedge_ratio</code> — the pairs-trading hedge ratio is the same OLS slope with a different x.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="compute_metrics computes Sharpe, CAGR, drawdown — no covariance against any benchmark appears there (that's the gap this lesson highlights).">In <code>analyze_equity.py:compute_metrics</code>.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="The VHDL module hardcodes four trained weights; it never estimates a slope from data on-chip.">In <code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code>.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="build_features computes returns, MAs, vol, RSI — no regression slopes.">In <code>paper_trading_sim.py:build_features</code>.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q3. A market-neutral fund returns +6% in a year the index drops −20%. A long-only fund returns +25% in a year the index gains +30% (β ≈ 1). Which showed more alpha?</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="+25% is the bigger NUMBER, but with β≈1 the market handed it +30% — its alpha is about −5%. It actually destroyed value relative to its exposure.">The long-only fund — +25% beats +6%.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Market-neutral: α ≈ 6% − 0×(−20%) = +6%. Long-only: α ≈ 25% − 1×30% = −5%. The neutral fund generated six points of return orthogonal to a crashing market; the long-only fund underperformed the exposure it carried. This is exactly why total return comparisons across different βs are meaningless.">The market-neutral fund — +6% with β≈0 is +6% alpha, while the long-only fund's alpha is roughly −5%.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="They differ by ~11 points of alpha — far from a tie. Compute each as r_s − β·r_m.">Roughly equal alpha.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Alpha is precisely the tool that makes this comparison possible — it puts both funds on an exposure-adjusted footing.">Alpha can't compare funds with different strategies.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q4. Sharpe vs alpha — a 2× leveraged S&amp;P index fund has, relative to the index itself…</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Leverage scales mean and volatility together, so the ratio (Sharpe) is unchanged — and an index position has no return orthogonal to the index.">Higher Sharpe and positive alpha.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. Leverage multiplies both the numerator (mean return) and denominator (vol) of Sharpe, leaving it ~unchanged (ignoring financing costs). And regressing 2×market on market gives β=2, α=0 — there is nothing orthogonal to the index in it. This is the cleanest demonstration that neither high returns nor decent Sharpe imply alpha.">About the same Sharpe and zero alpha (β = 2).</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Sharpe doesn't double — both mean and vol double, the ratio survives.">Double the Sharpe, zero alpha.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="β is 2 by construction; but alpha is 0, not negative (before fees/financing).">β = 2 and meaningfully negative alpha.</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<div class="question" data-question><p class="q">Q5. The repo's best bundle shows 27.19% annualized over 2020–2025 (long-only top-5 daily picks). Before celebrating, the single most important missing number is…</p><ul class="options">
<li data-correct="false" data-why="MaxDD matters and analyze_equity.py already reports it. The question is what's MISSING that contextualizes the return.">The maximum drawdown.</li>
<li data-correct="true" data-why="Right. 2020–2025 was a historic bull run; a long-only equity portfolio almost certainly carries β near 1, and β×market may account for most of the 27%. Regressing the equity curve's daily returns on SPY's (the ~10-line alpha_beta addition) splits the number into commodity (β×market) and skill (α). The same Sharpe could come from pure exposure or pure alpha — and an allocator pays very differently for the two.">Its β to the market over the same window — without it you can't say how much of the 27% was just the 2020–2025 bull market.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="More trees won't tell you whether the return was market exposure. The missing piece is attribution, not model capacity.">The model's n_estimators.</li>
<li data-correct="false" data-why="Training R² is nearly irrelevant to evaluating realized P&L — and is expected to be tiny on this target anyway.">The training R².</li>
</ul><p class="feedback" hidden></p></div>

<h2>5. Scenarios</h2>
<details class="scenario"><summary>S1. You add <code>alpha_beta()</code> to analyze_equity.py and the walk-forward strategy shows α = +9%/yr, β = 0.85, but the α's t-stat is 1.4. An allocator asks: "so do you have alpha or not?" What's the honest answer?</summary>
<p>"The point estimate is +9% but the data can't yet rule out zero." A t-stat of 1.4 means the α is 1.4 standard errors from zero — below the conventional ~2 threshold, so a luck-only explanation survives. This is the normal condition for young strategies: with daily data, the t-stat grows roughly with √years × (information ratio), so a true α of this size might need 6–10 years of live-like history to clear significance. What you CAN say honestly: the β is well-estimated (slopes pin down much faster than intercepts), so the strategy is demonstrably not just leveraged market; and the α estimate is positive across subperiods (show it). What you cannot say: "we have proven 9% alpha." Allocators have heard that one before — Lesson 0026's standard-error discipline is exactly what separates the credible pitch.</p></details>

<details class="scenario"><summary>S2. To make the strategy market-neutral, you short SPY futures sized at β = 0.85 of the book. Six months later the book is down on flat markets and the re-measured β of the *hedged* book is −0.4. What happened?</summary>
<p>The hedge overshot because β drifted — and you kept paying for stale neutrality. β is an estimate from a window, not a constant: the portfolio's composition changes daily (top-5 picks rotate through sectors with different betas), and the picks' own betas move with regimes. If the book's true β fell toward 0.45 while you stayed short 0.85-worth of futures, the net position is β ≈ −0.4: you're now short the market, and "down on flat markets" is the bleed of negative carry plus noise on an unintended directional bet. Fix discipline: (1) re-estimate β on a rolling window (e.g. 60–120 days) and rebalance the hedge on a schedule or a drift threshold; (2) measure the <em>hedged</em> book's β continuously — neutrality is verified, never assumed; (3) consider beta-forecasting rather than trailing estimation if turnover is high. Same non-stationarity lesson as rolling retrains and rolling hedge ratios — parameters in finance are subscriptions, not purchases.</p></details>

